1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,706
If the same Excel or CSV report arrives every week, stop treating the cleanup like a brand-new task.

2
00:00:06,706 --> 00:00:09,924
In Power Query, build the transformation once.

3
00:00:09,924 --> 00:00:16,820
Remove unwanted rows, rename columns, set data types, and standardize the fields you actually understand.

4
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:21,516
When the next file arrives in the same structure, replace the source and refresh.

5
00:00:21,516 --> 00:00:25,206
Power Query runs the same steps again in the same order.

6
00:00:25,206 --> 00:00:27,129
The real advantage is consistency.

7
00:00:27,129 --> 00:00:32,512
You are less likely to forget a step, and the transformation sequence stays visible.

8
00:00:32,512 --> 00:00:34,833
After the refresh, validate the result.

9
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:40,634
Compare row counts, check missing values, and make sure the expected columns are still there.

10
00:00:40,634 --> 00:00:42,653
A useful rule is simple.

11
00:00:42,653 --> 00:00:45,075
One-time cleanup can stay in Excel.

12
00:00:45,075 --> 00:00:47,902
Recurring structured cleanup belongs in Power Query.

13
00:00:47,902 --> 00:00:51,132
More complex multi-file logic may belong in Python.
